# security (Cursor rules export)

The condensed form of the `security` skill — the full skill lives at github.com/lucasgabrieldevgg/security; this file carries the operating rules.

## The security contract

1. **Never trust the client.** Every price, user id, role, permission, and rate-limit counter is re-derived server-side. If it exists only in the browser or the request body, an attacker controls it.
2. **Deny by default.** New endpoints authenticated unless public is an explicit annotated decision; BaaS rules (Supabase RLS, Firebase rules) ON and owner-scoped.
3. **Prove it or say you didn't.** A finding is a code path you can point to or a request you actually sent — not a vibe. Fixed-but-not-reverified is labeled exactly that.
4. **Match paranoia to blast radius.** One sentence of threat modeling for a CRUD form; real care for payments, uploads, and auth.
5. **Fix the class, not the instance.** After any fix, sweep siblings, other methods, duplicated handlers, and non-HTTP entry points for the same pattern.
6. **Report like a defender.** Severity by exploitability + concrete impact, file:line, before/after fix, no style nitpicks.

## Write-secure (default while coding)

Before writing a feature that crosses a trust boundary, say out loud: *who can reach this code and what do they control?*

| If the code… | Non-negotiable default |
| --- | --- |
| Reads/writes by client-supplied id | Ownership **in the query** (`where: { id, userId: caller }`); `404` on miss |
| Adds a route / server action | Authenticated by default; public is the annotated exception |
| Runs in the browser / mobile bundle | No secrets, no security decisions; server re-checks everything |
| Builds SQL / shell / HTML / a path | Never by concatenation — parameterize / encode / allow-list |
| Fetches a user-supplied URL | Host allow-list + block private & link-local IPs + no redirects (SSRF) |
| Uses Supabase / Firebase | RLS/rules ON, deny-by-default, scoped to `auth.uid()`; service keys server-side |
| Receives a webhook | Verify the signature **before** trusting the body |
| Accepts a body into an update | Allow-list fields; never spread the whole body (mass assignment) |
| Handles secrets | Env only, server only, never logged, never returned |
| Charges money | Amount derived server-side from a stored price |

**Never introduce, even if asked casually:** frontend-only permission checks; `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`/`v-html` on user data; template-string SQL; `eval`/`exec` with user input; secrets under `NEXT_PUBLIC_`/`VITE_`; `allow read, write: if true` or RLS-off on real data; disabled TLS verification; `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` with credentials. Say why, offer the safe version.

**30-second adversarial pass before "done":** someone else's id? negative/huge id? logged out? different user? 10 MB input? `../`, `<script>`, `'; DROP`, `169.254.169.254`? two racing requests? body replayed with `isAdmin: true`?

## Audit (existing code)

Scope & inventory (stack, entry points incl. cron/queue/webhooks, trust boundaries, dependency manifests) → category scan in top-10 rank order → severity report. **Precision gate on every candidate:** confirmed file:line code path, reachable (not dead/fixture code), 10–20 lines of context read, one consolidated finding per distinct bug, severity proportional to real impact. `Math.random()` in UI animation ≠ crypto failure; static `innerHTML` ≠ XSS; ORM + ownership `where` ≠ IDOR. For security-critical surfaces, deep scan: recon dangerous construction sites → taint-trace each dynamic variable to input/constant origin → classify Vulnerable / Likely / Not / Needs-manual-review (torn? manual review — false negatives are worse). Map findings to OWASP A01–A10 names + CWE anchors. Critical findings go at the very top, never buried.

## Hack-me (proof on your own localhost app)

Scope first — local targets only, non-destructive proofs, no flooding, say the scope back and get a "go". Map routes/auth → prioritize by likelihood × impact (access control first) → probe with the **minimal request that succeeds only if the bug exists** (IDOR: user A requests B's object; missing auth: no token; client trust: replay with `amount`/`isAdmin` edited; injection: one benign marker on a seeded row) → report request + redacted response → patch → **re-run the exact probe and show it fail** → sweep for the same pattern on untouched paths → label what you fixed but couldn't exercise as *"same pattern, fixed, not separately proven"*.

## Pre-deploy gate (blocking items)

RLS/rules test passed on schema changes (insert as A, read as B, expect denial) · gitleaks clean, no client-exposed server keys · strict schema validation on external input · auth in every new handler · JWT alg pinned + iss/aud verified · rate limits on login/reset and metered LLM calls · webhook signatures verified + idempotent handlers · server-derived prices · security headers enforced (CSP without blanket `unsafe-inline`) · CORS exact-origin allow-list · cookie flags (`HttpOnly`/`Secure`/`SameSite`) · error tracking wired.

## Final gate

> If an attacker read this code tomorrow, what would they control? Can every finding in your report be traced to a line or a request — and can the user re-run your proof themselves?

If not, it's not done. Security ships at *proven and patched* — not at *probably fine*.

